# ==================== SECTION II ====================
content.append("""## II Blacky Makes Sure / 布莱基去确认

===Original===
> As true as ever I've cawed a caw

That was a new-laid egg I saw.

"What are you talking about?" demanded Sammy Jay, coming up just in time to hear the last part of what Blacky the Crow was mumbling to himself.
"Oh nothing, Cousin, nothing at all," replied Blacky. "I was just talking foolishness to myself."
Sammy looked at him sharply. "You aren't feeling sick, are you, Cousin Blacky?" he asked. "Must be something the matter with you when you begin talking about new-laid eggs, when everything's covered with snow and ice. Foolishness is no name for it. Whoever heard of such a thing as a new-laid egg this time of year?"
"Nobody, I guess," replied Blacky. "I told you I was just talking foolishness. You see, I'm so hungry that I just got to thinking what I'd have if I could have anything I wanted. That made me think of eggs, and I tried to think just how I would feel if I should suddenly see a great big egg right in front of me. I guess I must have said something about it."
"I guess you must have. It isn't egg time yet, and it won't be for a long time. Take my advice and just forget about impossible things. I'm going over to Farmer Brown's corncrib. Corn may not be as good as eggs, but it is very good and very filling. Better come along," said Sammy.
"Not this morning, thank you. Some other time, perhaps," replied Blacky.
He watched Sammy disappear through the trees. Then he flew to the top of the tallest pine-tree to make sure that no one was about. When he was quite sure that no one was watching him, he spread his wings and headed for the most lonesome corner of the Green Forest.
"I'm foolish. I know I'm foolish," he muttered. "But I've just got to have another look in that old nest of Redtail the Hawk. I just can't get it out of my head that that was an egg, a great, big, white egg, that I saw there yesterday. It won't do any harm to have another look, anyway."

===Chinese===
> 我敢用我的每一声呱呱叫来发誓

我看到的是一颗刚下的蛋。

"你在说什么？"松鸦萨米问道，刚好赶上了乌鸦布莱基自言自语的最后一句话。
"哦，没什么，表哥，什么也没有，"布莱基回答。"我只是在跟自己说傻话罢了。"
萨米锐利地看了看他。"你该不是不舒服吧，布莱基表弟？"他问道。"你居然开始说什么刚下的蛋，到处都是冰天雪地的，你一定是有毛病了。说傻话都算轻的。谁听说过这个季节还能有新鲜的蛋？"
"大概没人听说过，"布莱基回答。"我跟你说过了，我只是在说傻话。你看，我实在太饿了，就忍不住想，要是想要什么就能有什么就好了。这就让我想到了蛋，我就试着想象，要是突然看到一颗大大的蛋就在我面前，我会是什么感觉。我猜我一定是说了些什么。"
"我猜你一定是说了。现在还不是下蛋的时候，离那时候还早着呢。听我的劝，忘了那些不可能的事吧。我要去布朗农夫的玉米仓了。玉米虽然不如蛋好吃，但也挺好的，很顶饱。不如一起走吧，"萨米说。
"今天早上就不去了，谢谢。也许改天吧，"布莱基回答。
他看着萨米消失在树林中。然后他飞到最高的松树顶上，确认周围没有别人。当他确信没有人在看他的时候，他张开翅膀，朝绿森林最偏僻的角落飞去。
"我很傻。我知道我很傻，"他嘀咕道。"但我就是得再去看看红尾鹰的那个旧巢。昨天我看到的那个，一颗大大的、白色的蛋，我怎么也忘不掉。再去看一眼也不会有什么坏处。"

===Original===
Straight toward the tree in which was the great tumbledown nest of Redtail the Hawk he flew, and as he drew near, he flew high, for Blacky is too shrewd and smart to take any chances. Not that he thought that there could be any danger there; but you never can tell, and it is always the part of wisdom to be on the safe side. As he passed over the top of the tree, he looked down eagerly. Just imagine how he felt when instead of one, he saw *two* white things in the old nest\u2014two white things that looked for all the world like eggs! The day before there had been but one; now there were *two*. That settled it in Blacky's mind; they were eggs! They couldn't be anything else.
Blacky kept right on flying. Somehow he didn't dare stop just then. He was too much excited by what he had discovered to think clearly. He had got to have time to get his wits together. Whoever had laid those eggs was big and strong. He felt sure of that. It must be someone a great deal bigger than himself, and he was of no mind to get into trouble, even for a dinner of fresh eggs. He must first find out whose they were; then he would know better what to do. He felt sure that no one else knew about them, and he knew that they couldn't run away. So he kept right on flying until he reached a certain tall pine-tree where he could sit and think without being disturbed.
"Eggs!" he muttered. "Real eggs! Now who under the sun can have moved into Redtail's old house? And what can they mean by laying eggs before Mistress Spring has even sent word that she has started? It's too much for me. It certainly is too much for me."

===Chinese===
他径直朝那棵长着红尾鹰那个巨大破旧鸟巢的树飞去。越靠近的时候，他飞得越高，因为布莱基太精明了，不会冒任何险。倒不是他觉得那里会有什么危险，而是你永远拿不准，小心谨慎总是上策。当他飞过树顶的时候，他急切地往下看。你猜他看到了什么——他看到旧巢里不是一颗，而是*两*个白色的东西！两个看起来完完全全就是蛋的东西！前一天还只有一颗，现在有了*两*颗！布莱基心里一下子确定了：那就是蛋！不可能是别的东西。
布莱基继续飞着。不知怎的，他不敢停下。他太兴奋了，激动得没法冷静思考。他需要时间把脑子理清楚。下那些蛋的一定是个又大又壮的家伙。他对此确信无疑。那一定比他自己大得多，他可不想惹麻烦，就算是一顿新鲜蛋的大餐也不值得。他必须先弄清楚那些蛋是谁的，然后再决定怎么做。他觉得没有别人知道那些蛋的事，而且他知道那些蛋也不会跑掉。所以他一直飞，飞到一棵高高的松树上，可以坐在那里安安静静地想事情，不会被打扰。
"蛋！"他嘀咕道。"真正的蛋！到底是谁搬进了红尾鹰的老房子？在春姑娘都还没传话说要来之前就下蛋，他们到底想干什么？这太不可思议了。确实太不可思议了。"
""")

# ==================== SECTION III ====================
content.append("""## III Blacky Finds Out Who Owns the Eggs / 布莱基查明蛋的主人

===Original===
Two big white eggs in a tumbledown nest, and snow and ice everywhere! Did ever anybody hear of such a thing before?
"Wouldn't believe it, if I hadn't seen it with my own eyes," muttered Blacky the Crow. "Have to believe them. If I can't believe them, it's of no use to try to believe anything in this world. As sure as I sit here, that old nest has two eggs in it. Whoever laid them must be crazy to start housekeeping at this time of year. I must find out whose eggs they are and then\u2014"
Blacky didn't finish, but there was a hungry look in his eyes that would have told any who saw it, had there been any to see it, that he had a use for those eggs. But there was none to see it, and he took the greatest care that there should be none to see him when he once again started for a certain lonesome corner of the Green Forest.

===Chinese===
破旧的鸟巢里有两颗大大的白蛋，到处都是冰雪！谁听说过这样的事？
"要不是亲眼看见，我才不信呢，"乌鸦布莱基嘀咕道。"但我不得不信。要是连这个都不信，那在这个世界上就没什么值得相信的了。我可以坐在这里发誓，那个旧巢里确实有两颗蛋。不管是谁下的蛋，在这个季节就开始安家，一定是疯了。我必须弄清楚那些蛋是谁的，然后——"
布莱基没有说完，但他眼中那种饥渴的神色，要是有人看到的话，一定会知道他对那些蛋另有打算。但周围没有别人看到，当他再次出发前往绿森林那个偏僻角落的时候，他格外小心，确保没有人看到他。

===Original===
"First I'll make sure that the eggs are still there," thought he, and flew high above the tree tops, so that as he passed over the tree in which was the old nest of Redtail the Hawk, he might look down into it. To have seen him, you would never have guessed that he was looking for anything in particular. He seemed to be just flying over on his way to some distant place. If the eggs were still there, he meant to come back and hide in the top of a nearby pine-tree to watch until he was sure that he might safely steal those eggs, or to find out whose they were.
Blacky's heart beat fast with excitement as he drew near that old tumbledown nest. Would those two big white eggs be there? Perhaps there would be three! The very thought made him flap his wings a little faster. A few more wing strokes and he would be right over the tree. How he did hope to see those eggs! He could almost see into the nest now. One stroke! Two strokes! Three strokes! Blacky bit his tongue to keep from giving a sharp caw of disappointment and surprise.
There were no eggs to be seen. No, sir, there wasn't a sign of eggs in that old nest. There wasn't because\u2014why, do you think? There wasn't because Blacky looked straight down on a great mass of feathers which quite covered them from sight, and he didn't have to look twice to know that that great mass of feathers was really a great bird, the bird to whom those eggs belonged.
Blacky didn't turn to come back as he had planned. He kept right on, just as if he hadn't seen anything, and as he flew he shivered a little. He shivered at the thought of what might have happened to him if he had tried to steal those eggs the day before and had been caught doing it.
"I'm thankful I knew enough to leave them alone," said he. "Funny I never once guessed whose eggs they are. I might have known that no one but Hooty the Horned Owl would think of nesting at this time of year. And that was Mrs. Hooty I saw on the nest just now. My, but she's big! She's bigger than Hooty himself! Yes, sir, it's a lucky thing I didn't try to get those eggs yesterday. Probably both Hooty and Mrs. Hooty were sitting close by, only they were sitting so still that I thought they were parts of the tree they were in. Blacky, Blacky, the sooner you forget those eggs the better."

> Some things are best forgotten

As soon as they are learned.

Who never plays with fire

Will surely not get burned.

===Chinese===
"我先去看看蛋还在不在，"他想，于是他飞到树顶上方很高很高的地方，这样当他飞过红尾鹰旧巢所在的那棵树时，就能从上面往下看。要是有人看到他，你绝对猜不到他是在找什么东西。他看起来就像只是路过，飞向某个远处的地方。如果蛋还在，他就打算回来藏在附近一棵松树的顶上，一直守着，直到确定可以安全地偷走那些蛋，或者弄清楚那些蛋是谁的。
当他靠近那个破旧鸟巢的时候，布莱基的心兴奋得怦怦直跳。那两颗大白蛋还在吗？也许会有三颗了！光是这么一想，他就忍不住多扇了几下翅膀。再扇几下，他就正好飞到那棵树的上方了。他多么希望看到那些蛋啊！现在他几乎都能看到巢里面了。一下！两下！三下！布莱基咬住舌头，才没有发出一声失望又惊讶的尖叫。
蛋一颗也看不到。没错，先生，那个旧巢里连一颗蛋的影子都没有。看不到蛋，因为——你猜为什么？因为布莱基往下看到的是一大团羽毛，把蛋完全遮住了。他不用看第二眼就知道，那一大团羽毛其实是一只大鸟，那些蛋的主人。
布莱基没有像计划的那样折回去。他继续往前飞，就好像什么也没看到似的。飞的时候，他微微打了个寒战。他想到要是昨天去偷蛋被当场抓住，后果会怎么样，就不禁浑身发抖。
"幸好我够聪明，没有去碰它们，"他说。"真奇怪，我居然一直没猜到那些蛋是谁的。我早该想到的，除了猫头鹰胡蒂，谁会在这个季节筑巢呢。刚才巢上的那个就是胡蒂太太。天哪，她可真大！比胡蒂自己还大呢！没错，先生，幸好我昨天没去偷那些蛋。很可能胡蒂和胡蒂太太当时就坐在旁边，只是他们坐得太安静了，我误以为他们是树的一部分。布莱基啊布莱基，你越快忘掉那些蛋越好。"

> 有些事情最好忘掉

刚知道就别再想。

不玩火的人

肯定不会被烧伤。
""")

# ==================== SECTION IV ====================
content.append("""## IV The Cunning of Blacky / 布莱基的诡计

===Original===
Now when Blacky the Crow discovered that the eggs in the old tumbledown nest of Redtail the Hawk in a lonesome corner of the Green Forest belonged to Hooty the Owl, he straightway made the best of resolutions; he would simply forget all about those eggs. He would forget that he ever had seen them, and he would stay away from that corner of the Green Forest. That was a very wise resolution. Of all the people who live in the Green Forest, none is fiercer or more savage than Hooty the Owl, unless it is Mrs. Hooty. She is bigger than Hooty and certainly quite as much to be feared by the little people.
All this Blacky knows. No one knows it better. And Blacky is not one to poke his head into trouble with his eyes open. So he very wisely resolved to forget all about those eggs. Now it is one thing to make a resolution and quite another thing to live up to it, as you all know. It was easy enough to say that he would forget, but not at all easy to forget. It would have been different if it had been spring or early summer, when there were plenty of other eggs to be had by anyone smart enough to find them and steal them. But now, when it was still winter (such an unheard-of time for anyone to have eggs!), and it was hard work to find enough to keep a hungry Crow's stomach filled, the thought of those eggs would keep popping into his head. He just couldn't seem to forget them. After a little, he didn't try.
Now Blacky the Crow is very, very cunning. He is one of the smartest of all the little people who fly. No one can get into more mischief and still keep out of trouble than can Blacky the Crow. That is because he uses the wits in that black head of his. In fact, some people are unkind enough to say that he spends all his spare time in planning mischief. The more he thought of those eggs, the more he wanted them, and it wasn't long before he began to try to plan some way to get them without risking his own precious skin.
"I can't do it alone," thought he, "and yet if I take anyone into my secret, I'll have to share those eggs. That won't do at all, because I want them myself. I found them, and I ought to have them." He quite forgot or overlooked the fact that those eggs really belonged to Hooty and Mrs. Hooty and to no one else. "Now let me see, what can I do?"
He thought and he thought and he thought and he thought, and little by little a plan worked out in his little black head. Then he chuckled. He chuckled right out loud, then hurriedly looked around to see if anyone had heard him. No one had, so he chuckled again. He cocked his head on one side and half closed his eyes, as if that plan was something he could see and he was looking at it very hard. Then he cocked his head on the other side and did the same thing.
"It's all right," said he at last. "It'll give my relatives a lot of fun, and of course they will be very grateful to me for that. It won't hurt Hooty or Mrs. Hooty a bit, but it will make them very angry. They have very short tempers, and people with short tempers usually forget everything else when they are angry. We'll pay them a visit while the sun is bright, because then perhaps they cannot see well enough to catch us, and we'll tease them until they lose their tempers and forget all about keeping guard over those eggs. Then I'll slip in and get one and perhaps both of them. Without knowing that they are doing anything of the kind, my friends and relatives will help me to get a good meal. My, how good those eggs will taste!"
It was a very clever and cunning plan, for Blacky is a very clever and cunning rascal, but of course it didn't deserve success because nothing that means needless worry and trouble for others deserves to succeed.

===Chinese===
当乌鸦布莱基发现绿森林偏僻角落里红尾鹰旧巢中的那些蛋是猫头鹰胡蒂的之后，他立刻下了一个最好的决心：干脆把那些蛋统统忘掉。他要忘掉自己曾经看到过它们，而且要远离绿森林那个角落。这是一个非常明智的决定。在所有住在绿森林里的动物中，没有谁比猫头鹰胡蒂更凶猛更可怕的了，除非是胡蒂太太。她比胡蒂更大，对小动物们来说当然也一样可怕。
这些布莱基都知道。没有人比他更清楚。而且布莱基可不是那种睁着眼睛往麻烦里钻的人。所以他非常明智地决定把那些蛋统统忘掉。不过嘛，正如大家所知道的，下决心是一回事，照着做又是另一回事。嘴上说忘掉很容易，但真正忘掉可不那么容易。如果是在春天或初夏，那就不一样了，那时候聪明的人只要会找、会偷，到处都能弄到蛋。但现在还是冬天——谁听说过这个季节会有蛋！——而且要填饱一只饥饿乌鸦的肚子本来就够难了，那两颗蛋的念头却不停地往他脑袋里钻。他怎么也忘不掉。过了一会儿，他也不想去忘了。
乌鸦布莱基是非常非常狡猾的。他是所有会飞的小动物中最聪明的一个。没有人能像乌鸦布莱基那样惹了那么多麻烦还能全身而退。这是因为他善于动用他那黑色脑袋里的小聪明。事实上，有些人毫不客气地说，他把所有的空闲时间都花在了盘算恶作剧上。他越想那些蛋，就越想要它们，没过多久，他就开始想办法要在不冒生命危险的情况下把它们弄到手。
"我一个人干不了，"他想，"但如果让别人知道我的秘密，我就得跟他们分那些蛋。那可不行，因为我自己想要。是我发现的蛋，应该归我。"他完全忘记或忽略了那些蛋实际上属于胡蒂和胡蒂太太，不属于任何人。"让我想想，我能怎么做呢？"
他想啊想啊想啊想啊，慢慢地，一个计划在他小小的黑色脑袋里成型了。然后他咯咯地笑了。他笑出了声，然后赶紧左右张望，看有没有人听到。没有人听到，于是他又笑了。他把脑袋歪到一边，半闭着眼睛，好像那个计划是一件他能看到的东西，他正在使劲盯着看似的。然后他又把脑袋歪到另一边，做了同样的动作。
"很好，"他最后说。"这会给我亲戚们带来很多乐趣，他们当然也会非常感激我的。这不会伤害胡蒂和胡蒂太太一丁点，但会让他们非常生气。他们的脾气很急，脾气急的人一生气就会把什么都忘了。我们趁阳光最亮的时候去拜访他们，因为那样他们可能看不清楚，抓不到我们。我们会戏弄他们，直到他们发脾气，忘了守着那些蛋。然后我就偷偷溜进去，拿走一颗，也许两颗都拿走。我的朋友们和亲戚们在不知不觉中就会帮我美餐一顿。天哪，那些蛋该多好吃啊！"
这是一个非常聪明狡猾的计策，因为布莱基本来就是一个非常聪明狡猾的坏家伙，但这个计策当然不该成功，因为任何给他人带来不必要的烦恼和麻烦的事情都不该得逞。
""")

with open(path, 'a', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(''.join(content))

print(f"Sections II-IV appended. File size: {os.path.getsize(path)} bytes")
